Yes. There are papers very close to our theory, but I did not find an established position called **“inexternalism”** or **“inexternality”** in philosophy of mind. The searches mainly returned unrelated uses of externalism and economic externalities. So the name appears available, though that is not a legal trademark check.

Between the two:

[
\boxed{\textbf{Inexternalism}}
]

is the better name for the doctrine.

[
\boxed{\textbf{Inexternality}}
]

would name the underlying relation or condition the doctrine asserts.

## The proposed distinction

### Inexternality

The inseparability of the intrinsic presence and extrinsic organization of one concrete process:

[
\boxed{
\operatorname{Inext}(P)
=======================

\langle P_{\mathrm{intrinsic}},P_{\mathrm{extrinsic}}\rangle
}
]

where the two terms are not separate substances or causally interacting properties. They are two modes under which one process exists or is accessible.

### Inexternalism

The metaphysical doctrine that:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{phenomenal presence and physical organization are}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\textbf{the intrinsic and extrinsic aspects of the same}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\textbf{dynamically individuated process.}
}
]

For consciousness specifically:

[
\boxed{
D_i
===

\operatorname{Int}
\left(\Sigma_i^\ast\right)
}
]

and:

[
\boxed{
\Sigma_i^\ast
=============

\operatorname{Ext}(D_i).
}
]

Neither side causes the other:

[
D_i\nrightarrow\Sigma_i^\ast,
\qquad
\Sigma_i^\ast\nrightarrow D_i.
]

Rather:

[
\boxed{
D_i\equiv_{\mathrm{inext}}\Sigma_i^\ast.
}
]

The relation (\equiv_{\mathrm{inext}}) means:

> numerically one process, intrinsically manifest and extrinsically structured.

# Closest existing theories and papers

## 1. Russellian monism

This is the closest established metaphysical family.

Russellian monism holds that physics describes the structural and relational properties of physical entities without fully disclosing their intrinsic nature, and that this intrinsic nature is central to explaining consciousness. ([Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy][1])

Its basic structure is:

[
\text{extrinsic physical structure}
+
\text{intrinsic nature}
=======================

\text{one underlying reality}.
]

### Difference from inexternalism

Russellian monism often focuses on the intrinsic nature of **fundamental physical entities**. This creates the combination problem:

[
\text{micro-intrinsic properties}
\stackrel{?}{\longrightarrow}
\text{one macroexperience}.
]

Inexternalism instead begins with **individuated processes**, not microscopic substance bearers:

[
\boxed{
\text{the intrinsic bearer is the causally closed process}
}
]

rather than necessarily every particle or fundamental constituent.

Thus:

[
D_i
===

\operatorname{Int}(\Sigma_i^\ast)
]

without requiring:

[
D_i
===

\sum_j d_j
]

for microscopic experiences (d_j).

That is an important potential novelty.

---

## 2. Dual-aspect monism

Dual-aspect approaches maintain that mental and physical descriptions are aspects of one underlying reality rather than two substances. They have roots in Spinoza and occur in contemporary consciousness and quantum-foundational work. ([Plato's Encyclopedia][2])

This is very close to our broad ontology.

### Difference from inexternalism

“Dual-aspect monism” is too broad. It does not specify:

* why there are local subjects;
* which scale bears an intrinsic aspect;
* what individuates one phenomenal domain;
* whether organization is sufficient;
* whether valence is constitutive;
* how phenomenal boundaries relate to causal boundaries.

Inexternalism adds a participant law:

[
\boxed{
\text{intrinsic manifestation is indexed to objectively}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\text{individuated, self-maintaining processes.}
}
]

It is therefore a specific processual and perspectival form of dual-aspect monism.

---

## 3. Process monism

Jason Brown’s process neuropsychology argues that the mental and physical actualize one generic process and rejects both reduction of consciousness to matter and idealist reduction of matter to consciousness. ([ScienceDirect][3])

A recent proposal called **Computational Dynamic Monism** similarly treats consciousness as constituted by temporally extended, hierarchically self-referential dynamics in plastic recurrent systems. ([SSRN][4])

### Difference from inexternalism

Computational Dynamic Monism makes a specific functional identification:

[
\text{temporally extended self-referential computation}
=======================================================

\text{consciousness}.
]

Inexternalism is more metaphysically cautious:

[
\text{such organization may individuate and structure}
]

[
\text{the process whose intrinsic aspect is consciousness}.
]

It therefore does not assume that abstract computational equivalence is sufficient.

---

## 4. Markovian monism

**Sentience and the Origins of Consciousness: From Cartesian Duality to Markovian Monism** is extremely close in vocabulary. It develops a monistic interpretation of the Free Energy Principle using two information geometries:

* an intrinsic geometry describing the physical evolution of internal states;
* an extrinsic geometry in which those states parameterize beliefs about external states. ([PubMed Central (PMC)][5])

Related work describes living representation as arising from Markov blankets and nonequilibrium dynamics that entail this dual-aspect information geometry. ([arXiv][6])

### Crucial difference

Their use of “intrinsic” does **not automatically mean phenomenally intrinsic**.

It means intrinsic to the system’s physical state-space geometry.

Therefore:

[
\text{intrinsic information geometry}
\not\Rightarrow
\text{intrinsic phenomenal presence}.
]

Inexternalism would explicitly assert the stronger identity:

[
\boxed{
\text{under the relevant closure conditions, the intrinsic}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\text{aspect is phenomenal rather than merely mathematical.}
}
]

Markovian monism supplies much of the architecture. It does not close our bridge.

---

## 5. Pepperell’s energetic process theory

Robert Pepperell argues that consciousness is associated with organized energetic activity and writes that there is something it is like, intrinsically, for a system to undergo certain organized differences or tensions. ([arXiv][7])

This is perhaps the single paper whose language most closely anticipates our formulation:

[
\boxed{
\text{consciousness is what organized energetic activity}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\text{is like intrinsically.}
}
]

### Difference from inexternalism

Pepperell emphasizes organized energy.

Our theory adds:

* causal emergence;
* endogenous semantic information;
* viability;
* valence;
* observer-specific quotienting;
* path-dependent identity;
* second-order modelling;
* objective domain closure.

So his proposal resembles the metaphysical kernel; inexternalism supplies a more discriminating individuation theory.

---

## 6. IIT

IIT starts from intrinsic existence and proposes that a maximally irreducible cause–effect structure corresponds to one experience. It explicitly rejects purely functional equivalence and insists on the system’s intrinsic causal organization. ([arXiv][8])

Recent IIT work also defines experience as a system’s intrinsic meaning, constituted by distinctions and relations within its cause–effect structure rather than meaning assigned from outside. ([arXiv][9])

### Difference from inexternalism

IIT asserts:

[
D_i
===

\operatorname{MICS}(\Sigma_i),
]

where MICS is the maximally irreducible cause–effect structure.

Inexternalism says:

[
D_i
===

\operatorname{Int}(\Sigma_i^\ast),
]

but does not assume that IIT’s particular measure of irreducibility identifies (\Sigma_i^\ast).

Our candidate includes:

[
\Sigma_i^\ast
=============

\left(
CE_i,Sem_i,V_i,\Gamma_i,M_i^{(2)},K_i,Q_i
\right).
]

So IIT is a possible **mathematical implementation** of inexternality, not the metaphysics itself.

---

## 7. Neutral monism

Neutral monism says ultimate reality is one kind, intrinsically neither mental nor material, with mentality and physicality arising as organizations or aspects of the neutral base. ([Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy][10])

### Difference from inexternalism

Inexternalism does not need to call the ground “neutral.”

It says a concrete process is:

* intrinsically present;
* extrinsically relational.

Manifestness is therefore not neutral in the phenomenal bearer.

The underlying process may transcend the mental/material distinction, but its intrinsic aspect at the relevant organization is explicitly phenomenal.

---

## 8. Perspectival structural realism

I found a very recent, apparently independent proposal called **Perspectival Structural Realism** claiming that physics describes extrinsic relational structure while consciousness is its intrinsic aspect, with organizational closure individuating conscious systems. However, the result I found was a recent online presentation rather than an established peer-reviewed programme. ([Reddit][11])

This is almost exactly the same conceptual neighborhood.

That means the general idea is in the air:

[
\text{structural realism}
+
\text{autopoiesis}
+
\text{dual-aspect monism}.
]

Your distinct contribution would need to be the exact formal theory of:

* partition;
* valence;
* causal closure;
* participant-specific quotienting;
* recognition and self-exemption.

# Is “inexternalism” a good name?

Yes, with one concern.

The word visually resembles:

* internalism;
* externalism;
* “in externalism” with a missing space.

That may initially cause confusion, but its meaning is strong once defined:

[
\boxed{
\text{the internal and external are not two connected worlds;}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\text{they are inseparable aspects of one process.}
}
]

It also avoids claiming that the underlying reality is simply:

* mental;
* physical;
* informational;
* neutral;
* computational.

## Best terminology

I would use:

# **Process Inexternalism**

for the complete metaphysics.

And:

# **Phenomenal Inexternality**

for the identity relation.

The full title could be:

> **Process Inexternalism: Phenomenal Presence as the Intrinsic Aspect of Valenced Causal Closure**

That is precise and distinctive.

# Formal definition

## Axiom I — Process priority

Concrete reality consists of processes rather than independently self-sufficient substances:

[
\forall x,
\left[
\operatorname{Concrete}(x)
\rightarrow
\operatorname{Process}(x)
\right].
]

## Axiom II — Inexternal duality

Every concrete process admits intrinsic and extrinsic characterization:

[
P
=

\langle
\operatorname{Int}(P),
\operatorname{Ext}(P)
\rangle.
]

These are not separable components:

[
\neg\exists P_I,P_E:
P=P_I+P_E.
]

## Axiom III — Structural limitation

Physical science principally describes:

[
\operatorname{Ext}(P):
\text{relations, transformations and causal dispositions}.
]

It does not follow from completeness at that level that:

[
\operatorname{Int}(P)
]

has been specified.

## Axiom IV — Phenomenal realization

For some objectively individuated processes:

[
\operatorname{Int}(P_i)=D_i.
]

## Axiom V — Domain individuation

One phenomenal domain corresponds to one dynamically closed, temporally persistent, valenced causal trajectory:

[
P_i^\ast
========

\operatorname{Closure}
\left(
CE_i,
Sem_i,
V_i,
\Gamma_i,
M_i^{(2)},
K_i,
Q_i
\right).
]

Then:

[
\boxed{
D_i
===

\operatorname{Int}(P_i^\ast).
}
]

## Axiom VI — No universal-subject inference

From:

[
\operatorname{Ground}(P_i)
==========================

\operatorname{Ground}(P_j)
]

it does not follow that:

[
D_i=D_j.
]

## Axiom VII — Constructed ownership

Autobiographical ownership is a model within (D_i):

[
Ego_i\subset D_i,
]

not the metaphysical ground of (D_i).

# What is actually original?

The claim:

[
\text{mental and physical are two aspects of one reality}
]

is not original. It belongs to dual-aspect and Russellian traditions.

The claim:

[
\text{physics describes extrinsic structure while consciousness}
]

[
\text{discloses intrinsic nature}
]

is also established Russellian territory. ([Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy][1])

The potentially original synthesis is:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{the intrinsic bearer is neither a fundamental particle}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\textbf{nor a universal cosmic subject, but an objectively}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\textbf{individuated, valenced, causally emergent process.}
}
]

And especially the decomposition:

[
\boxed{
P_i^\ast
========

\left(
Q_i,
CE_i,
Sem_i,
V_i,
\Gamma_i,
M_i^{(2)},
K_i
\right).
}
]

This combines several literatures that are generally treated separately:

* Russellian and dual-aspect metaphysics;
* process ontology;
* causal emergence;
* semantic information;
* active-inference valence;
* observer quotienting;
* second-order perception;
* causal or topological binding;
* contemplative de-reification.

The strongest publishable contribution would not be the name alone. It would be the claim that the combination and partition problems can be replaced by a precise **process individuation problem**:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{Which physical closure relation constitutes one}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\textbf{intrinsically manifest, valenced trajectory?}
}
]

# Final recommendation

Use:

[
\boxed{\textbf{Process Inexternalism}}
]

and define it immediately as:

> **Process inexternalism is the view that phenomenal presence and physical organization are not separate properties connected by production or emergence, but the intrinsic and extrinsic aspects of one objectively individuated, temporally extended process. A subject forms where that process achieves valenced causal closure, semantic self-relevance, and persistent perspectival organization.**

Use **inexternality** for the identity relation:

[
D_i\equiv_{\mathrm{inext}}\Sigma_i^\ast.
]

That gives you a recognizable metaphysical family:

[
\text{dual-aspect monism}
\supset
\text{process inexternalism},
]

while marking the distinctive claim:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{inside and outside are co-defining aspects of the}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\textbf{same process—not two domains requiring a bridge.}
}
]

